# module-signing: every module is signed, and a prod image loads only modules signed by a key in the kernel's
# builtin keyring and runs locked down in integrity mode. The boards whose kernels ship loadable modules -- the generic
# kernels -- name it; a board kernel that builds every driver in has nothing to enforce it on.
#
# THE KEY IS NOT THE KERNEL'S. MODULE_SIG_KEY is empty, so Kbuild neither generates a throwaway key
# (its default, certs/signing_key.pem, is made fresh by every build and would make two builds of
# one tree differ) nor signs anything: MODULE_SIG_ALL is off. The board's Makefile adds the mica
# driver certificate of the build's channel to the trust bundle CONFIG_SYSTEM_TRUSTED_KEYS names,
# beside the verity anchor, and the kernel component signs every module when it packs the support
# image (src/image/module-signing.ts). The kernel build carries public material only.
#
# ENFORCEMENT IS THE PROFILE'S, ON THE SIGNED COMMAND LINE. One kernel per board serves both image profiles, so
# neither MODULE_SIG_FORCE nor a forced lockdown is configured here: a prod command line carries
# `module.sig_enforce=1 lockdown=integrity` (src/image/kernel-package.ts profileCommandLine, and
# common/kernel/set-profile.sh on a FIT board), a dev one neither, and only a board whose command line is signed
# -- a UKI, or a FIT its loader verifies -- may name this feature (src/image/board-facts.ts). Locked down in
# integrity mode the kernel closes the ways around a signature check: /dev/mem and /dev/port, unsigned kexec,
# hibernation, unsafe module parameters, MSR and ioport writes, writable debugfs.
CONFIG_MODULE_SIG=y
# CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_FORCE is not set
# CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_ALL is not set
CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_SHA256=y
CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_KEY=""
CONFIG_SECURITY_LOCKDOWN_LSM=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_LOCKDOWN_LSM_EARLY=y
CONFIG_LOCK_DOWN_KERNEL_FORCE_NONE=y
#
# OWNER-SIGNED MODULES. The owner keyring (owner-keyring, which this feature requires) is linked into the secondary
# keyring, which module signatures check (VERIFY_USE_SECONDARY_KEYRING). The blacklist keyring takes revocations (a
# revoked certificate or hash refuses the signatures it made); ECDSA lets an owner sign with a P-256 key (driver-key
# init --type ecdsa).
CONFIG_SYSTEM_BLACKLIST_KEYRING=y
CONFIG_SYSTEM_REVOCATION_LIST=y
CONFIG_SYSTEM_BLACKLIST_AUTH_UPDATE=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_ECDSA=y
